 

DR Congo Cardano Community Call 4 Building a Stronger Local Community

On August 25, 2026, the Cardano community in the Democratic Republic of Congo came together once again for the fourth edition of the DR Congo Cardano & Web3 Community Call.

For me, this fourth edition was more than just another online meeting. It was another opportunity to connect people, share knowledge and demonstrate that the Cardano ecosystem is gradually building a stronger presence at the local level.

With participants joining from Goma, different parts of the DRC and neighboring Rwanda, the session brought together people interested in blockchain, Web3, governance, artificial intelligence and community development.

A Community Growing Step by Step
The DR Congo Cardano Community Call started in May 2026 with a simple objective: create a regular space where people in the DRC can discover Cardano, learn together and find ways to contribute to the ecosystem.

Four editions later, the initiative continues to grow.

The fourth edition reached up to 24 participants, showing that there is growing interest in Cardano and Web3 within the local community.

What is particularly encouraging is that the conversations do not stop when the call ends. Following this edition, six new members joined our community WhatsApp group, creating an opportunity for continued discussions, learning and collaboration.

For a community still developing, these small steps matter.



My Contribution: Presenting the Goma Hub
One of the highlights of the session for me was the opportunity to present the Goma Hub, its vision and its activities.

I  clearly stated that Goma Hub operates in four areas:

1. Education in blockchain and information technology.

2. Sustainable environment.

3. Leveraging blockchain for conservation.

4. Development of the local ecosystem.

The Goma Hub is part of our effort to create a local environment where people can learn about blockchain and emerging technologies while connecting these technologies with real community needs.

Our vision goes beyond simply talking about Cardano.

We want to contribute to:

Blockchain and Web3 education
Community development
Environmental sustainability
Technology and innovation
Local participation in the Cardano ecosystem
The presentation was also an opportunity to explain how local initiatives can become a bridge between the global Cardano ecosystem and communities that are still discovering blockchain technology.

Understanding Cardano: Starting with the Fundamentals
The second presentation was delivered by Martin Musagara, who introduced participants to the fundamentals of Cardano.

For newcomers, starting with the basics is essential.

The presentation covered Cardano as a decentralized and open-source blockchain, the role of ADA, Proof of Stake and staking.

The discussion also introduced participants to Cardano's governance model, including DReps, the Constitution and Project Catalyst.

This was particularly useful because many people hear about Cardano, ADA or Catalyst without necessarily understanding how the different parts of the ecosystem work together.

Education therefore remains one of the most important foundations for community growth.






Connecting the Community with Current Cardano Opportunities
Olivier Mwatsimulamo then brought participants closer to the latest developments within the ecosystem.

His presentation focused on Cardano news, opportunities and ways for community members to participate.

Among the subjects discussed were Project Catalyst, governance, Intersect, interoperability between Cardano and Injective, and developments around Leios.

The session also included an important reminder about wallet security.

As adoption grows, educating users about security becomes increasingly important. Community members need to know how to identify suspicious communications and verify information through official channels before taking sensitive actions.

This is one area where local education can have a real impact.

AI and Web3: From Ideas to Action
Another interesting part of the session was the presentation by Boaz Bandu on AI and Web3 in practice.

The discussion focused on something very practical: how artificial intelligence can help people move from an idea to a more structured project.

AI can help newcomers:

understand difficult concepts;
organize information;
develop ideas;
create educational content;
research possible solutions; and
structure early-stage projects.
But one message stood out to me:

AI should be used as a tool, not as a replacement for people and communities.

A good idea still needs people with knowledge, experience and the willingness to build.

The combination of AI + human expertise + community collaboration can therefore become a powerful way to support innovation in emerging Web3 communities.

What the Community Told Us
One of the most valuable parts of the Community Call was the discussion at the end.

Participants did not simply listen. They also shared suggestions about how we can improve future activities.

One proposal was to organize X Spaces, allowing people outside Goma and those who cannot attend regular sessions to participate in the conversations.

Another important idea was to consider restarting an onboarding program for newcomers.

These suggestions are important because community building requires continuous feedback.

We need to ask ourselves not only:

“What can we teach?”

but also:

“What does the community actually need?”

That distinction can make our future activities more relevant and impactful.




Learning Should Continue After the Call
A Community Call should not be the end of someone's learning journey.

During the session, participants were also introduced to educational resources that can help them continue exploring Cardano.

Among them was the French-language resource:

“Getting Started with Cardano: Essential Resources to Find Your Way in the Ecosystem.”

Resources like this are particularly important for communities where English-language technical documentation can sometimes be a barrier for newcomers.

Making knowledge more accessible means giving more people the opportunity to move from simply hearing about Cardano to actually participating in the ecosystem.

From Participants to Contributors
For me, one of the most important lessons from this fourth edition is that community growth should eventually lead to contribution.

It is good to have people attending events.

It is even better when participants begin asking questions, learning new skills, joining discussions and eventually contributing their own ideas and expertise.

Someone can start as a newcomer and eventually become:

a community educator;
a developer;
a content creator;
a researcher;
a governance participant;
a project builder; or
a community leader.
This is the kind of progression we want to encourage in the DRC.

The Road Ahead for Cardano in the DRC
The Democratic Republic of Congo has a large and diverse population, a growing interest in digital technologies and many young people looking for opportunities to learn and innovate.

There is therefore a strong opportunity to develop more local initiatives around blockchain and Web3 education.

But this will require patience and consistency.

We need more:

Education → Community → Collaboration → Contribution → Building.

The role of local hubs and community members is to help create the environment where this progression can happen.

The Goma Hub is committed to being part of that journey.

What's Next?
The fourth Community Call is now behind us, but the work continues.

We will continue exploring ways to:

welcome newcomers;
organize educational activities;
create spaces for discussion;
connect local members with global Cardano opportunities;
encourage contribution; and
strengthen collaboration within the DRC and beyond.
The next DR Congo Cardano Community Call is scheduled for:

📅 September 29, 2026

🕡 6:30 PM – 7:30 PM

We look forward to welcoming both returning participants and newcomers.

Final Thoughts
The DR Congo Cardano & Web3 Community Call #4 reminded me that building a blockchain ecosystem is not only about technology.

It is also about people, education, trust, collaboration and consistent community work.

Every participant who joins, every question that is asked, every new person who starts learning and every community member who decides to contribute represents another step forward.

We may still be at an early stage, but the direction is encouraging.

The Cardano community in the DRC is growing — one conversation, one learner and one contribution at a time. 



Learn. Connect. Contribute. Build.



